            {
                "entry_id": "256d56b4-6cc8-33ad-b52e-bb6af18fbc40",
                "word": "ébionite",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "0350363c-072e-4226-a8a2-6e31fe693360",
                        "entry_id": "256d56b4-6cc8-33ad-b52e-bb6af18fbc40",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "ébionites"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                        "entry_id": "256d56b4-6cc8-33ad-b52e-bb6af18fbc40",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Membre d’un groupe religieux judéo-chrétien marginal attesté, à partir de la seconde moitié du IIᵉ siècle, par les écrits d'Irénée de Lyon et d'Hippolyte de Rome."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bcfa5dd5-44a3-43e2-9215-ed5431af73d4",
                                "sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                                "text": "Il faut renoncer à en faire, comme Tischendorf le voulait, un ébionite, fût-ce un ébionite orthodoxe."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9c9a9e88-8879-4958-9e8a-e0585bc57a3c",
                                "sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                                "text": "De plus, comme tous les hétérodoxes judéochrétiens, ils [les ébionites] se caractérisent par un antipaulinisme* affirmé et virulent [*opposition aux doctrines de Paul de Tarse, notamment sur l'ouverture du christianisme aux non juifs]."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7dbb3472-8980-4b57-ac5e-d99da0cd4e00",
                                "sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                                "text": "Pour J.M. Magnin, les membres de la première communauté hiérosolymitaine [ou Église de Jérusalem], que leurs compatriotes juifs appelaient nazaréens, ont très bien pu se donner à eux-mêmes le nom d'ébionites — c'est-à-dire « les pauvres ». … Nulle part, toutefois, il n'est rapporté de manière claire dans les sources que les nazôréens se sont donné le nom d’ébionites."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "495e1eb9-ee4b-4712-a30f-ae3ddb39dc68",
                                "sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                                "text": "Si l'on veut atteindre sous leur forme la plus primitive les traits communs à l'essénisme et au christianisme, c'est chez les Ébionites qu'on doit de préférence les rechercher du côté chrétien."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "dcbbc9d8-0a4c-48c5-b0b1-36a6074addb0",
                        "source_entry_id": "256d56b4-6cc8-33ad-b52e-bb6af18fbc40",
                        "source_sense_id": "9f9bfb7a-5fb6-3b7b-a235-5a29e0c57bfd",
                        "target_lang_word": "Ebionite",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Ebionite",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c11cfe53-1a0a-3078-bc3c-14e3bace790d",
                                        "entry_id": "7ecbdc2d-86e9-36b0-93d7-6ac755b235da",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A member of an early Jewish Christian sect that lived in and around Judea and Palestine from the 1st to the 4th century."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "07986f8e-6a1b-445a-931e-fb8977a2d296",
                                                "sense_id": "c11cfe53-1a0a-3078-bc3c-14e3bace790d",
                                                "text": "And if the pious bishop and martyr, St. Irenaeus, happen to drop any thing pointed against the Doctor's progenitors, the ancient Ebionites, of heretical memory, he gently passes it over as though no such passages occurred in that learned and venerable Father's writings."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "47ff16cd-f0cb-4dfb-92f0-13aa3b87f68c",
                                                "sense_id": "c11cfe53-1a0a-3078-bc3c-14e3bace790d",
                                                "text": "EBION, the author of the heresy of the Ebionites, was a disciple of Cerinthus and his successor."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "40060f76-7f59-44f8-998b-1c7cf0e34832",
                                                "sense_id": "c11cfe53-1a0a-3078-bc3c-14e3bace790d",
                                                "text": "The passage was so taken by the Jewish or Ebionite translators, Aquila, Theodotion, and Symmachus."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "04e78877-f086-401a-8586-83c36428b231",
                                                "sense_id": "c11cfe53-1a0a-3078-bc3c-14e3bace790d",
                                                "text": "* Jesus teaches vegetarianism. Epiphanius describes the Ebionites as vegetarians, and in the Recognitions and Homilies vegetarianism is strongly connected to the rejection of animal sacrifice and is required of all the followers of Jesus. When Epiphanius questions a Jewish Christian as to why he was a vegetarian, the Jewish Christian responds simply: “Christ revealed it to me” (Panarion 30.18.9)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
